#!/usr/bin/env bash
#
# archive-pin -- report or advance this repository's pin on the archive submodule.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS
#
# `.gitmodules` sets `ignore = all` on the archive submodule, so a stale pin is
# reported by neither `git status` nor `git diff`. A gitlink is not a file
# either, so no pre-commit hook is ever handed one. Every instrument this
# repository runs normally is blind to this drift.
#
# WHY ADVANCING THE PIN CAN BE AUTOMATED AT ALL
#
# The archive is append-only: a corpus is added, never edited (AGENTS.md, "The
# archive is frozen, and corrections land in the suite"). That makes a bump
# mechanical, but only while the invariant actually holds. This script verifies
# it and refuses to move the pin if it does not.
#
# HOW THE CHANGE SET IS ENUMERATED
#
# One `git diff --raw -z --ignore-submodules=none`, not a hand-rolled tree walk.
# A walk that classifies entries by object type silently skips a GITLINK, and
# rebuilding paths by hand mangles the names git quotes and any name carrying
# trailing whitespace -- each of which drops a subtree at BOTH pins, so nothing
# is reported and that subtree is exempt from the freeze permanently.
#
# `--ignore-submodules=none` IS LOAD-BEARING AND MUST NOT BE DROPPED. The
# archive's own `.gitmodules` sets `ignore = all` on every one of its nested
# submodules, and per gitmodules(5) that setting suppresses them from `diff`.
# Without the flag, repointing a nested corpus submodule is invisible here --
# the same defect this script exists to correct, one level down.
#
# `--raw` rather than `--name-status` because the mode is the only thing that
# distinguishes a file from a gitlink or a symlink at a given path, and the
# classification below turns on exactly that.
#
# `--no-renames` IS ALSO LOAD-BEARING. Git detects renames by DEFAULT, with no
# flag and no configuration, and a detected rename emits TWO NUL-terminated
# paths in one record instead of one. That desynchronises the meta/path read
# pairing below for every record after it, silently -- the misparsed fields fail
# the mode and status tests quietly rather than erroring. The malformed-record
# check in the loop is the second line of defense; this flag is the first.
#
# WHAT COUNTS AS A VIOLATION
#
#   a corpus present at the old pin, changed in any way   -> refused
#     ("corpus" means <org>/<second segment>, which may be a bare file such as
#      ethereum/EXTRACTION.md, not necessarily a directory)
#   a corpus present at the old pin, removed              -> refused
#   a NEW <org>/<corpus>                                  -> allowed, the point
#   a top-level FILE added or modified                    -> allowed, reported
#   a top-level file DELETED, or changed type             -> refused
#   a top-level entry that is not a file (gitlink etc.)   -> refused
#   CLAUDE.md / AGENTS.md / .gitmodules at the top level  -> refused without
#                                                            --allow-guarded
#   .github/**  (the archive's own CI)                    -> allowed, reported
#   .claude/**  (loaded as agent instructions)            -> refused without
#                                                            --allow-guarded
#   a gitlink or symlink anywhere under either            -> refused
#
# Top-level files are allowed by DEFAULT rather than by an allow-list, because
# an allow-list there fails closed on the archive's real records (`.gitignore`,
# `LICENSE`, `.pre-commit-config.yaml` and others all change legitimately) and
# a single unlisted one then blocks every future bump, which is how a gate
# stops being used. Deletions and type changes are still refused, so the
# looseness cannot remove the provenance ledger or swap a record for a gitlink.
#
# CLAUDE.md and AGENTS.md are guarded because this repository deliberately does
# NOT exclude `archive/` from `claudeMdExcludes`, so they reach an agent's
# context; `.gitmodules` because it can repoint a nested submodule at another
# host. All three change legitimately when a corpus is recorded -- hence a flag,
# rather than a refusal you cannot get past.
#
# Anything instruction-shaped is guarded at ANY depth: a `.claude/` segment
# anywhere in the path, or a basename of `CLAUDE.md` or `AGENTS.md`. Claude Code
# loads a nested `.claude/CLAUDE.md` and `.claude/rules/*.md` as project
# instructions when a file beside them is read, which is why `archive/` being
# deliberately absent from this repository's `claudeMdExcludes` matters.
#
# THE GUARD IS PROSPECTIVE, and saying so is the honest form. Measured at the
# recorded pin, this archive carries exactly two instruction files, both at the
# top level, and `archive/.claude/` holds only `settings.json` -- so nothing
# currently exercises the deeper rule. Its value is that a corpus arriving with
# instruction files is NAMED rather than silent. It cannot close the class: a
# new corpus may legitimately carry a `CLAUDE.md`, and admitting new corpora is
# the whole purpose of the archive. `--allow-guarded` is the deliberate path.
#
# USAGE
#
#   tools/archive-pin --check           report drift; exit 1 if the pin is behind
#   tools/archive-pin                   verify, advance the pin, commit
#   tools/archive-pin --no-commit       verify and stage, but do not commit
#   tools/archive-pin --to <sha>        target a specific commit, not the tip
#   tools/archive-pin --allow-guarded   permit CLAUDE.md/AGENTS.md/.gitmodules
#   tools/archive-pin --force           skip the fast-forward requirement only
#
# `--force` does NOT weaken the freeze check. Nothing does.
#
# ARCHIVE_LOCAL_CLONE may name an absolute path to a local clone of the archive,
# used only to avoid refetching objects over the network. Content is addressed
# by SHA, so this cannot substitute content; it is still required to be an
# absolute directory path.
# --- end usage ---

set -euo pipefail

SUBMODULE=archive
BRANCH=main
CONFIG_DIRS=(.claude .github)
GUARDED=(CLAUDE.md AGENTS.md .gitmodules)

die()  { printf '%s: %s\n' "${0##*/}" "$*" >&2; exit 1; }
note() { printf '  %s\n' "$*"; }
has()  { local n=$1; shift; local x; for x in "$@"; do [ "$x" = "$n" ] && return 0; done; return 1; }
is_objectname() { [[ ${1-} =~ ^[0-9a-f]{7,40}$ ]]; }
is_filemode()   { case "${1-}" in 100644|100755) return 0 ;; *) return 1 ;; esac; }
# Instruction-shaped at ANY depth. Guarding only the top level left
# `<org>/CLAUDE.md` and `<org>/.claude/**` classified as ordinary corpus content.
is_instruction_path() {
  case "$1" in .claude/*|*/.claude/*) return 0 ;; esac
  case "${1##*/}" in CLAUDE.md|AGENTS.md) return 0 ;; esac
  return 1
}

MODE=bump COMMIT=yes FORCE=no ALLOW_GUARDED=no TARGET=

while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do
  case "$1" in
    --check)          MODE=check ;;
    --no-commit)      COMMIT=no ;;
    --force)          FORCE=yes ;;
    --allow-guarded)  ALLOW_GUARDED=yes ;;
    --to)             TARGET="${2:-}"; [ -n "$TARGET" ] || die "--to needs a commit"; shift ;;
    -h|--help)        sed -n '3,/^# --- end usage ---$/p' "$0" | sed 's/^#\{1,2\} \{0,1\}//; $d'; exit 0 ;;
    *)                die "unknown argument: $1" ;;
  esac
  shift
done

# An option-shaped value must never reach a git command: git parses options
# after positional arguments, so `--upload-pack=<command>` in a remote or
# refspec slot runs that command.
[ -z "$TARGET" ] || is_objectname "$TARGET" || die "--to takes a hex object name, got: $TARGET"

ROOT=$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel) || die "not inside a git repository"
cd "$ROOT" || die "could not enter $ROOT"
[ -e "$SUBMODULE/.git" ] || die "$SUBMODULE is not initialised -- run: git submodule update --init $SUBMODULE"

URL=$(git config -f .gitmodules --get "submodule.$SUBMODULE.url") || die "no url recorded for submodule.$SUBMODULE"
# `.gitmodules` is tracked, so a pull request can change this value.
case "$URL" in
  https://github.com/*) : ;;
  *) die "refusing a submodule url outside https://github.com/: $URL" ;;
esac

# --literal-pathspecs because `--` stops OPTION parsing, not PATHSPEC MAGIC
# parsing: a path beginning with `:` is read as magic and silently rewritten, so
# the existence probe below would answer "absent" for a real corpus and classify
# an edit to it as a brand-new one.
# Relative values are refused rather than resolved: this check would run in
# $ROOT while the fetch runs via `git -C archive`, one directory deeper, so a
# relative path would be validated and fetched from two different places.
CLONE="${ARCHIVE_LOCAL_CLONE:-$ROOT/../archive-reference-material}"
case "$CLONE" in
  *::*) die "refusing a transport-shaped ARCHIVE_LOCAL_CLONE: $CLONE" ;;
  /*)   : ;;
  *)    die "ARCHIVE_LOCAL_CLONE must be an absolute path: $CLONE" ;;
esac

sub() { git --literal-pathspecs -C "$SUBMODULE" "$@"; }
CURRENT=$(git rev-parse "HEAD:$SUBMODULE") || die "no gitlink recorded at $SUBMODULE"

if [ -z "$TARGET" ]; then
  TARGET=$(git ls-remote --end-of-options "$URL" "refs/heads/$BRANCH" | awk '{print $1}')
  [ -n "$TARGET" ] || die "could not resolve refs/heads/$BRANCH at $URL"
fi
# Re-checked after resolution: `checkout` and `log` below cannot take
# --end-of-options, so this is what keeps their positional uses safe.
is_objectname "$TARGET" || die "resolved target is not an object name: $TARGET"

printf '%s\n' "archive pin"
note "recorded : $CURRENT"
note "target   : $TARGET"
if [ "$CURRENT" = "$TARGET" ]; then note "status   : at the tip, nothing to do"; exit 0; fi
note "status   : BEHIND"
[ "$MODE" = check ] && { printf '\nThe pin is stale. Advance it with: tools/archive-pin\n' >&2; exit 1; }

# Refuse before touching anything, so no refusal has to be rolled back.
OTHER=$(git diff --cached --ignore-submodules=none --name-only | grep -v "^$SUBMODULE\$" || true)
[ -z "$OTHER" ] || die "unrelated changes are staged; commit or reset them first:"$'\n'"$OTHER"

if ! sub cat-file -e "$TARGET^{commit}" 2>/dev/null; then
  if [ -d "$CLONE" ] && { [ -d "$CLONE/.git" ] || [ -f "$CLONE/HEAD" ]; }; then
    note "fetching : $TARGET from $CLONE"
    sub fetch --no-tags --quiet --end-of-options "$CLONE" "$TARGET" 2>/dev/null || true
  fi
fi
if ! sub cat-file -e "$TARGET^{commit}" 2>/dev/null; then
  note "fetching : $TARGET from $URL"
  sub fetch --no-tags --quiet --end-of-options "$URL" "$TARGET" 2>/dev/null \
    || sub fetch --no-tags --quiet --end-of-options "$URL" "$BRANCH"
fi
sub cat-file -e "$TARGET^{commit}" 2>/dev/null || die "target $TARGET is not reachable after fetching"

if [ "$FORCE" = no ]; then
  sub merge-base --is-ancestor "$CURRENT" "$TARGET" \
    || die "$TARGET does not descend from the recorded pin -- the archive history was rewritten. Inspect it, then re-run with --force if that is intended."
fi

# ------------------------------------------------------------ freeze check --
printf '\nfreeze verification\n'
TMP=$(mktemp) || die "could not create a temporary file"
APPLYING=
rollback() {
  git restore --staged -- "$SUBMODULE" 2>/dev/null || git reset -q -- "$SUBMODULE" 2>/dev/null || true
  sub checkout --no-recurse-submodules --quiet "$CURRENT" 2>/dev/null || true
}
# Interrupting between the checkout and the commit would otherwise leave the
# submodule moved and the bump staged, invisible to a plain `git status`.
# Disarms itself first: shared across EXIT and a terminating signal, this handler
# would otherwise run twice per interrupt, since it exits and that re-enters EXIT.
on_exit() { local rc=$?; trap - EXIT INT TERM
            if [ -n "$APPLYING" ]; then rollback; fi; rm -f "$TMP"; exit "$rc"; }
trap on_exit EXIT INT TERM

# Not a process substitution: a failure inside one is swallowed, and the
# consuming loop cannot tell a truncated scan from a finished one.
sub diff --no-renames --raw -z --ignore-submodules=none --end-of-options "$CURRENT" "$TARGET" >"$TMP"

# Reads the tree ENTRY, so it answers correctly for a gitlink whose target
# object this repository does not have -- unlike `cat-file -e`, which errors
# there and would be read as "absent", i.e. as a brand-new corpus.
present_at_current() {
  local out
  out=$(sub ls-tree "$CURRENT" -- "$1") || die "could not read '$1' at $CURRENT"
  [ -n "$out" ]
}

violations=0 additions=0 recs=0 admins=0 guards=0 total=0
declare -A corpus_seen=()
guarded_paths=""

while IFS= read -r -d '' meta && IFS= read -r -d '' p; do
  total=$((total + 1))
  # Every --raw record starts with ':'. If one does not, the stream has
  # desynchronised and every field after it is read from the wrong entry.
  case "$meta" in :*) : ;; *) die "malformed diff record, refusing: $meta" ;; esac
  read -r _srcmode dstmode _srcsha _dstsha st <<<"${meta#:}"
  case "$p" in */*) top=${p%%/*} ;; *) top=$p ;; esac

  if [ "$top" = "$p" ]; then                        # a top-level entry
    if [ "$st" = D ] || [ "$st" = T ]; then
      note "TOPLEVEL $st $p   (a top-level record may be added or edited, not removed or retyped)"
      violations=$((violations + 1))
    elif ! is_filemode "$dstmode"; then
      note "TOPLEVEL $st $p   (mode $dstmode -- not a regular file; a gitlink or symlink here is not a record)"
      violations=$((violations + 1))
    elif has "$p" "${GUARDED[@]}"; then
      guards=$((guards + 1)); guarded_paths="$guarded_paths $p"; note "GUARDED  $st $p"
    else
      recs=$((recs + 1)); note "record   $st $p"
    fi
    continue
  fi

  if has "$top" "${CONFIG_DIRS[@]}"; then
    # A gitlink or symlink here is neither configuration nor an instruction
    # file; a deletion carries mode 000000, so it skips the mode test only.
    if [ "$st" != D ] && ! is_filemode "$dstmode"; then
      note "CONFIG   $st $p   (mode $dstmode -- not a regular file)"
      violations=$((violations + 1))
    elif is_instruction_path "$p"; then
      guards=$((guards + 1)); guarded_paths="$guarded_paths $p"; note "GUARDED  $st $p"
    else
      admins=$((admins + 1)); note "admin    $st $p"
    fi
    continue
  fi

  rest=${p#*/}; corpus="$top/${rest%%/*}"
  # Memoised: the answer is the same for every file in a corpus, and probing per
  # file costs one subprocess per file on an addition that can run to 30k of them.
  if [ -z "${corpus_seen[$corpus]+x}" ]; then
    if present_at_current "$corpus"; then
      corpus_seen[$corpus]=frozen; violations=$((violations + 1)); note "CHANGED  $corpus"
    else
      corpus_seen[$corpus]=new; additions=$((additions + 1)); note "added    $corpus"
    fi
  fi

  # Order matters here. An EXISTING corpus is frozen entire, so an instruction
  # file inside one is a freeze violation already recorded above -- it must not
  # be downgraded to "guarded", which --allow-guarded would then wave through.
  # Inside a NEW corpus the file is admitted but NAMED, because a corpus may
  # legitimately carry one and silence is the thing worth removing.
  if [ "${corpus_seen[$corpus]}" = new ] && is_instruction_path "$p"; then
    if [ "$st" != D ] && ! is_filemode "$dstmode"; then
      note "CONFIG   $st $p   (mode $dstmode -- not a regular file)"
      violations=$((violations + 1))
    else
      guards=$((guards + 1)); guarded_paths="$guarded_paths $p"; note "GUARDED  $st $p"
    fi
  fi
done <"$TMP"

note "paths compared: $total    corpora added: $additions    corpora changed: $violations    records: $recs    admin: $admins    guarded: $guards"

# Calibration. Differing trees must yield at least one path; zero means the
# comparison failed to look, not that the freeze held.
if [ "$(sub rev-parse "$CURRENT^{tree}")" != "$(sub rev-parse "$TARGET^{tree}")" ] && [ "$total" -eq 0 ]; then
  die "the two pins have different trees but the comparison reported no path at all -- it is broken, refusing to bump"
fi
if [ "$guards" -gt 0 ] && [ "$ALLOW_GUARDED" = no ]; then
  die "guarded file(s) changed:${guarded_paths}. Review the diff, then re-run with --allow-guarded."
fi
[ "$violations" -eq 0 ] || die "freeze violated: $violations path(s) edited, removed or retyped. The archive is append-only; investigate before bumping."
note "freeze holds"

# ----------------------------------------------------------------- apply it --
printf '\napplying\n'
APPLYING=1
sub checkout --no-recurse-submodules --quiet "$TARGET" -- || die "could not check out $TARGET"
git add -- "$SUBMODULE" || die "could not stage the bump"

staged=$(git diff --cached --ignore-submodules=none --name-only)
[ "$staged" = "$SUBMODULE" ] || die "expected only '$SUBMODULE' staged, got: ${staged:-<nothing>}"
note "staged   : $(git ls-files --stage -- "$SUBMODULE" | awk '{print $2}')"

if [ "$COMMIT" = no ]; then
  APPLYING=; note "left staged as requested; commit it yourself"; exit 0
fi

# C0 control bytes, and the bidi overrides that let a rendered subject differ
# from its bytes, would otherwise land verbatim in published history.
summary=$(sub log --format=%s -1 "$TARGET" \
  | tr -d '\000-\010\013\014\015\016-\037\177' \
  | LC_ALL=C sed -e 's/\xe2\x80[\x8e\x8f\xaa-\xae]//g' -e 's/\xe2\x81[\xa6-\xa9]//g' \
  | git stripspace)
[ -n "$summary" ] || summary="(no subject)"

git commit --quiet -F - <<EOF || die "the commit was rejected; the bump has been rolled back"
chore(archive): advance the pin to $(printf %.11s "$TARGET")

$summary

Append-only verified across the bump by \`git diff --raw --ignore-submodules=none\`
between the two pins: $total path(s) compared, $additions corpus/corpora added,
0 changed, 0 removed. $recs top-level record(s), $admins archive-config path(s),
and $guards guarded path(s) moved.${guarded_paths:+
Guarded:${guarded_paths}}

The comparison is calibrated: differing trees reporting no path at all is
treated as a broken scan and refused, not as a held freeze.

Recorded by tools/archive-pin. The gitlink is invisible to \`git status\` and
\`git diff\`, which is why this is verified rather than eyeballed.
EOF

APPLYING=
note "committed: $(git log --oneline -1)"
